/* =====================================================================
   LAYER 3 — THE OLD DRAINS
   Sewer band, the hinge. Register: the poison of the upper mains thins
   out and something older takes its place — this is where the chemistry
   stops being chemistry. Oldest masonry; the city's trunk mains were
   laid on top of these. Hub = TheGreatSink. Enter from L2 (TheSiphon);
   descend to L4 (DrownedHall) via TheOlderStair; ascend back to L2
   (Confluence) via the gate (TheChokedArch) and TheCorkscrew.
   Template: Ascent, Descent, Gate, Rest, 3 Caches/Puzzle, 2 Snares,
   1 combat, organic filler. Depths registered in setup.depthOf (= 3).
   ===================================================================== */


/* ---------- HUB ---------- */

:: TheGreatSink [upper]
<<nobr>>
The old stair lets you out at the lip of a basin so wide your lamp can't find
the far side of it — a great sink where a dozen ancient drains all empty at once,
their mouths ringed around the dark like the bells of so many drowned trumpets.
The brick here is not the city's brick. It is older, bigger, set without mortar by
hands that knew a thing about water the city forgot, and the water knows it: it
moves through here slow and deliberate, the way a thing moves through a house it
owns.
<</nobr>>

<<if $corruption gte 70>>
You stand at the rim and feel, distinctly, welcomed. That is the worst of it —
not that the place is wrong, but that the wrongness has started to feel like the
shape of a key turning in you, finding the matching shape already cut.
<<elseif $corruption gte 35>>
The chemical sting of the mains above is gone from the air down here, and you find
you miss it: it was at least a thing you understood. What's replaced it has no
smell you can name and no name you'd want to say aloud in the dark.
<<else>>
This is older than the sewer, and the sewer was old. You have the sudden, useless
certainty that the city above is the new thing, the thin recent crust, and that
this — the patient water, the lightless mouths — is what was always really here.
<</if>>

The drains run off in every direction, going up toward a cold you can almost
remember and down toward one you'd rather not.

<<link "Toward the way back up — but an arch has come down across it">><<goto "TheChokedArch">><</link>>
<<link "An older stair still, winding down">><<goto "TheOlderStair">><</link>>
<<link "A side-drain, hung with offerings">><<goto "TheVotiveDrain">><</link>>
<<link "A weir, choked pale with debris">><<goto "TheBoneWeir">><</link>>
<<link "A chamber of old carving, off the north mouth">><<goto "TheInscription">><</link>>
<<link "A stinking side-channel, off west">><<goto "TheTanneryDrain">><</link>>
<<link "Back up the worn stair, toward the trunk mains">><<goto "TheSiphon">><</link>>


/* ---------- ASCENT / DESCENT ---------- */

:: TheCorkscrew [upper]
A corkscrew stair, cut as a tight spiral around a central well, climbing up out of
the old drains toward the trunk mains. With every turn the air sweetens by a
degree — less of the cold mineral nothing below, more of the honest reek of rust
and rot and chemistry, which down here counts as the smell of home and safety, of
a world that at least runs on rules you can poison yourself by.

The well at the centre is the thing you keep not looking down. The spiral wraps
it close, each tread cantilevered out over a drop that goes back down into exactly
the dark you are leaving, and the wet treads show two sets of wear — one cupped by
feet going up, the way you are going, and one cupped deeper by feet going down. You
do not let yourself count which set is worn smoother.

Your lungs open. The light, when it finds you a few turns up, is the grey
borrowed daylight of the mains, and you climb toward it like a diver toward the
silver underside of the surface.

<<link "Up the last turns, into the trunk mains">><<goto "Confluence">><</link>>
<<link "Back down to the choked arch">><<goto "TheChokedArch">><</link>>


:: TheOlderStair [deep]
The stair down from the great sink is older even than the sink — undressed stone
worn into shallow bowls, the treads spaced for a stride that wasn't human, going
down into a dark that has stopped pretending to be a sewer at all. The water that
weeps down the walls here is clear, and cold, and entirely without smell, and that
absence is somehow worse than any stench the mains could offer.

A few steps down and the spacing tells on you. You have to break your own stride
to take the treads, lengthen it past where a leg wants to go, so that the descent
becomes a thing you do wrong on purpose, over and over, the stone teaching your
body a gait that isn't yours. The bowls worn into each tread are too deep to have
been worn by men, and too smooth to have been worn by anything that walked.

This is the way into the drowned places, where the masonry gives out and the
flooded galleries begin. You have the distinct sense, standing at the top of it,
that the stair is less a thing you descend than a thing that swallows, slowly, and
in no hurry, because where would you go.

<<link "Down, into the flooded dark">><<goto "DrownedHall">><</link>>
<<link "Sideways, into branching galleries">><<goto "TheSumpGalleries">><</link>>
<<link "Back up to the great sink">><<goto "TheGreatSink">><</link>>


/* ---------- GATE ---------- */

:: TheChokedArch [upper]
The way up runs under a great arch that has half come down — a fall of ancient
brick choking the passage to a low squeeze, and worse, a pocket of something heavy
and invisible has settled into the low place the way cold settles into a cellar.
Your lamp gutters and shrinks as you near it. The air there is dead; one breath of
it at the bottom of the squeeze and your head goes light and the dark crowds in at
the edges of your sight.

<<if $archCleared>>
But the choke is breathing now — you worked the old counterweight upstream, and
the sluice it governs has lifted the standing level just enough to flush the dead
air out of the low place and float you over the worst of the fall. The squeeze is
passable, and almost clean.

<<link "Through, and up the corkscrew stair">><<goto "TheCorkscrew">><</link>>
<<else>>
There's a counterweight-house off to the side — the old mechanism that once
governed the sluices through here. Work it right and you might vent the choke and
raise the water to carry you over. Or you can simply go down into the dead air and
force the squeeze, and breathe what's pooled there, and pay for it.

<<link "To the counterweight-house, to work the old mechanism">><<goto "TheCounterweight">><</link>>
<<set _tlbl to "Force the squeeze through the dead air (" + setup.tollTag("head", "filth", 12) + ")">><<link _tlbl>>
  <<corrupt "head" "filth" 12>>
  <<goto "TheCorkscrew">>
<</link>>
<</if>>
<<link "A dry vault, off the side">><<goto "TheLimeVault">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the great sink">><<goto "TheGreatSink">><</link>>


/* ---------- CACHES / PUZZLE ---------- */

:: TheCounterweight [upper]
The counterweight-house is a tall slot of a room full of seized iron: a great
stone block hung on a chain gone to orange lace, ringed by a sequence of pawls and
ratchets and a sluice-gate cranked shut a century ago and never opened since.
Nothing here wants to move.

Up close the iron is more wreck than machine — the chain has wept itself into a
single rusted column, the ratchet-teeth furred orange, the whole works fused by a
hundred years of nobody. But the logic of it is still legible, if you read the
wear: drop the pawls in order, let the block fall by stages, and the gate it
governs will lift and let the standing water through. Some engineer's hand cut the
sequence into the frame in a tidy stencilled script, sure once that whoever came
after would want the drains to keep working.

You are, you suppose, whoever came after. The silence in the slot is the particular
silence of a thing that has waited a long time to be of use again, and is in no
hurry, because it has already outlasted everyone who knew its name.

<<if $archCleared>>
The block hangs slack at the bottom of its travel, its work done. Downstream you
can still hear the choke breathing where you set it free.
<<else>>
<<linkreplace "Throw the pawls in order; let the counterweight fall">>
<<set $archCleared to true>><<set _f to random(2, 4)>><<set _got to setup.gainCoins(_f)>>
The block goes down in three slow shrieking stages, and on the third the old
sluice lifts with a groan you feel in your teeth, and far off under the arch the
dead air sighs out and the water comes up to take its place. The silt-pan under
the mechanism gives up <<if _got gt 0>><<= _got>> green coins as it tips, the toll of whoever last
stood here. You take them. Down here, coin spends.<<else>>a scatter of green coins as it tips, the toll of whoever last
stood here — but they curdle to rubbish in your grip, worthless to a hand as cursed as yours.<</if>>
<</linkreplace>>
<</if>>

<<link "Back to the choked arch">><<goto "TheChokedArch">><</link>>


:: TheVotiveDrain [upper]
<<corrupt "torso" "filth" 1>>
A narrow side-drain, and someone — many someones, across a very long time — has
made it a wishing-well. Coins are pressed into the wet mortar of the walls, knotted
to old nails by rotted thread, dropped in their hundreds into the silt at the
bottom: pennies and pfennigs and pieces with no nation's face at all, each one a
wish dropped down a drain by a hand reaching for a god that lived in the right
direction, which is to say *down*.

Closer, you start to read the wishes. Not in words — there are no words — but in
the way each coin was placed: some thumbed flat into the mortar with care, some
flung, some tied on with a knot you can tell was tied by someone weeping. A milk
tooth on a thread. A wedding band gone green. Each one is a small held breath, a
bargain struck in the dark by a hand that had run out of the upward kinds of god
and come looking down here for one that answers.

And the drain has the listened-to stillness of a place that has been talked to
like this for so long, and did answer, at least once. You find you are being careful where
you put your feet, the way you'd be careful in a church — and you keep your voice
down, without deciding to, in case the thing all those coins were dropped to is
still in, and still in the habit of listening.

<<if $votiveLooted>>
You've taken what you came to take. The rest of the wishes you leave to whatever
they were addressed to.
<<else>>
Coin is pressed into the wet mortar and knotted to old nails and dropped in its
hundreds into the silt — wishes addressed downward, each one there for whoever comes
back to //receive//, rather than collect, them.
<</if>>

<<link "Further in, toward a weeping grate">><<goto "TheWeepingGrate">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the great sink">><<goto "TheGreatSink">><</link>>


:: TheDeadLetterDrain [upper]
A drain where things wash in and never wash out: the silted dead-end where the old
system gave up its current and let the water stand and drop whatever it carried.
At the doorway it is only a heap, the grey undifferentiated sludge of every dead-end
down here, and you almost turn back before the heap resolves.

Then it resolves, and you wish it hadn't. The silt is a strata of the lost — a
child's shoe, a ring of keys, a sodden brick of envelopes fused into one grey loaf,
all the addresses run to nothing, a hundred years of dead letters the city flushed
and forgot it owed an answer to. You can read the layers like a riverbank: each
flood that ever reached this far laid down its losses and moved on, and none of it
ever left, because this is where leaving stops.

The water here does not move at all. It has the perfect flatness of water that has
forgotten how, and standing over it you understand the drain is not a place things
pass through on the way to somewhere — it is the somewhere, the address every lost
thing was secretly directed to, and your own two footprints, pressed fresh in the
silt behind you, are only the latest delivery the drain has signed for.

<<if $deadLetterLooted>>
You've turned it over already. Only the keys and the dead letters remain, and
those you leave; they were never going anywhere, and now neither were you.
<<else>>
Coin is salted through the strata of the lost, under the child's shoe and the ring of
keys and the sodden brick of dead letters fused into one grey loaf — there for whoever
digs it, when the thought of it walks them back this way.
<</if>>

<<link "An overflow tank, through the silt">><<goto "TheCisternOverflow">><</link>>
<<link "Back toward the lime vault">><<goto "TheLimeVault">><</link>>


/* ---------- REST ---------- */

:: TheLimeVault [upper]
Off the side of the choke there is a dry room, and dry is a miracle down here. It
was a lime-store once — the old works kept quicklime to sweeten the worst of the
flow — and a drift of the white powder still lines the floor and the long shelves,
caustic and bitter and, crucially, *hateful to rot*. Nothing wet grows here.
Nothing soft lives here. The air bites your throat and your eyes water, and it is
the cleanest air you have breathed since the sack came down.

Deeper in, the shelves go back further than the doorway promised, ranked with
burst sacks and the chalk-pale outlines where bags long crumbled left their shadow
on the stone. Here and there in the white drift are the clean small bones of things
that crawled in to die and were kept instead of taken — a rat curled tidy as a
fossil, a bird gone to lace — held perfect by the same hatred of rot that makes the
room safe. The lime does not save them. It only refuses to let them rot, which down
here you are coming to understand is a different and lonelier thing.

You sit with your back to a shelf of bagged lime that has set hard as stone, and
the dust stings the cuts on your hands honestly, like the world used to, and the
shaking goes out of you by degrees. For a little while, in the one room down here
the rot can't enter, you are nobody's and nothing's.
<<if $hp lt $maxhp>><<run setup.passTime(setup.REST_TIME)>><</if>><<set $hp to $maxhp>>

<<link "A silted dead-end drain, beyond the far door">><<goto "TheDeadLetterDrain">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the choked arch">><<goto "TheChokedArch">><</link>>


/* ---------- SNARES (bad ends) ---------- */

:: TheWeepingGrate [upper]
The drain narrows to a dead face of stone, and set into it at the height of your
own face is a grate — a small barred window into a deeper dark, its iron weeping a
slow rust-red that has stained the wall beneath it the shape of a long tongue. From
behind the bars comes a draft, and on the draft a voice: not loud, not threatening,
only patient and terribly kind, and it is saying your name. Not the name on your
papers. The other one. The one you only ever say to yourself.

//There you are,// it says, through the bars, in the dark, in your own voice worn
soft. //I've kept your place. Just tell me which of us is on which side of the
grate, and I'll let you through to the easier part. You've earned the easier part.//

It is the oldest trick the dark has, and you know it is a trick, and the knowing
does nothing to the wanting, because the wanting was here long before you and will
be here long after, waiting at the grate for exactly one tired and solitary fool.

<<link "Answer it. Tell it which side you're on.">><<goto "EndingGrate">><</link>>
<<link "Say nothing. Back away from the grate.">>
  <<soothe 4>>
  <<goto "TheVotiveDrain">>
<</link>>


:: TheWarmSilt [deep]
The gallery bottoms out in a bay of silt, and the silt is *warm* — blood-warm,
body-warm, a heat that has no business in this cold deep and asks no permission to
feel wonderful. It gives under your boot like a made bed.

You crouch and put your hand to it before you've decided to, and the warmth comes
up through your palm the way warmth comes up off a sleeping animal, even and slow
and somehow breathing. There is a depression near the middle, you notice, a long
shallow trough pressed into the silt the exact length of a lying person, its edges
gone smooth — as if someone rested here before you and the bed remembers the shape,
and has kept it ready, and faintly warm, against the next tired thing to come this
far down.

After the standing cold of the drowned places, after the ache the deep has worked
into every joint of you, the urge to simply lie down in it, to let the warmth come
up around you and take the weight of being upright and afraid, is enormous, and
patient, and exactly your size.

<<linkreplace "Lie down. Just for a moment. Just rest.">>
<<corrupt "torso" "filth" 28>>
You lie back into the warmth and it closes over you soft as silt and warm as a
mouth, and the rest is so total and so undeserved that you are nearly under before
you understand the heat for what it is: not the ground's, but *yours* — your own
heat, leaving you, drawn down into the patient warm dark a degree at a time, and
the silt no colder for the taking. You drag yourself up out of it on the last of
your alarm. You leave something of yourself in the bed-shape behind you. It keeps
your warmth. It will keep your place.
<</linkreplace>>

<<link "Back to the branching galleries">><<goto "TheSumpGalleries">><</link>>


/* ---------- COMBAT ---------- */

:: TheCrawl [upper]
Behind the bone-weir a crawl opens in the old brick, shoulder-width, going back and
down. The smell that comes out of it is not the chemical reek of the mains nor the
cold nothing of the deep but something between — a hot animal stink, a den-smell,
ammonia and milk and rot.

<<if $crawlCleared>>
The crawl is quiet now, its tenant dealt with, only the den-stink lingering and a
litter of cracked pale things you choose not to examine. The way through opens onto
the silted dead-end drain beyond.

<<link "On, to the dead-letter drain">><<goto "TheDeadLetterDrain">><</link>>
<<link "Back out to the weir">><<goto "TheBoneWeir">><</link>>
<<else>>
Something is denned in there, and as your light reaches in it comes to meet you —
not a rat. Longer. Pale as a root, blind, its skin the wet white of a thing grown
in the dark and never finished, with a mouth that opens too wide and too eagerly
for a head that has no eyes to aim it. A drain-crawler. It has heard you for some
time, and it is glad.

<<scentof "crawler">>
<<link "Meet it in the mouth of the crawl">>
  <<run setup.startFight("crawler", "TheCrawlCleared")>>
  <<goto "Combat">>
<</link>>
<<link "Back out to the weir, quietly">><<goto "TheBoneWeir">><</link>>
<</if>>


:: TheCrawlCleared [upper]
/* lands here after the crawler fight; sets the cleared flag, then folds onward */
<<set $crawlCleared to true>>
The crawler comes apart in the throat of the crawl, pale and wet and wrong to the
last, and you push past what's left of it on your hands and knees through a stink
that will be in your clothes for the rest of your life, however long that proves.
The crawl opens out beyond into a silted dead-end where the old water gave up.

<<link "On, to the dead-letter drain">><<goto "TheDeadLetterDrain">><</link>>
<<link "Back out to the weir">><<goto "TheBoneWeir">><</link>>


/* ---------- PASSAGE / ATMOSPHERE FILLER ---------- */

:: TheBoneWeir [upper]
A low weir crosses the drain here, and the slow water has built against it a reef
of everything it carried and couldn't lift over: a pale felted mass of bone and
root and rag and the small light architecture of a thousand dead things, combed out
of the flow and woven by the current into something that looks, in the lamplight,
disquietingly *made*.

<<if $weirGolemCleared>>
What rose out of the reef is spilled muck again, sliding back down the weir in slow
grey sheets to be only debris once more — which is the lie it told the first time,
and the lie the next solitary traveller will believe. The way past it is open.

<<link "A bricked outfall, off the basin">><<goto "TheOldOutfall">><</link>>
<<link "A low crawl, behind the weir">><<goto "TheCrawl">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the great sink">><<goto "TheGreatSink">><</link>>
<<else>>
It is only debris. You tell yourself it is only debris, right up until the reef
*gathers* — until the bone and the rag and the rope of matted hair haul themselves
up off the weir into the rough shape of a man twice your size, and you understand
the thing the old galleries keep trying to teach you: that down here the filth does
not merely rot, it *agrees*. A thousand discarded and individually harmless things,
given enough dark and enough time, will pool their small separate ruins into one
shared appetite — and the appetite is so much larger than the sum of what fed it. A
shit-golem. The drain, standing up.

<<scentof "shitgolem">>
<<link "Put it down before it finishes standing">>
  <<run setup.startFight("shitgolem", "TheBoneWeirCleared")>>
  <<goto "Combat">>
<</link>>
<<link "Back to the great sink, while it's still gathering">><<goto "TheGreatSink">><</link>>
<</if>>


:: TheBoneWeirCleared [upper]
/* lands here after the shit-golem fight; sets the flag, then folds onward */
<<set $weirGolemCleared to true>>
You break the will before you break the body — and the instant it goes, the thing
is only weight again: the golem sloughs apart all at once, a collapsing reef of bone
and rag and rope-end slumping back down the weir, every piece of it individually
harmless, the whole of it suddenly and finally just *the drain* again. What it had
swallowed and not been able to digest glitters in the wet slope of what's left.

<<link "Pick through what it leaves">><<goto "TheOldOutfall">><</link>>
<<link "A low crawl, behind the weir">><<goto "TheCrawl">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the great sink">><<goto "TheGreatSink">><</link>>


:: TheOldOutfall [upper]
For one swimming moment it is hope: a great round outfall mouth in the wall, the
classic shape, the shape that in the world above means *a river, a daylight, an
out*. You go to it the way you'd go to a door in a burning house. And it is bricked.
Sealed flush with old engineering brick, mortared tight, a hundred years or three
hundred, by people who decided very deliberately that whatever this outfall once
let out should never let out again.

You put your hand flat on the brick and feel, or imagine you feel, a faint warmth
on the far side, and the sealed mouth gives back nothing but the small cold fact of
itself: there was a way here once, and someone walled it, and they were not walling
to keep *you* out.

<<if not $outfallVoiceHeard>>
Your hand is still on the warm brick when the warmth speaks — a voice on the far side, soft and
close through the mortar, with the small wrong eagerness of a thing that wants very much for you to
do a particular thing.
<<linkreplace "Press your ear to the warm brick. Something is breathing on the far side">><<nobr>><<set $outfallVoiceHeard to true>>
//Warm, isn't it,// it says. //That's the morning, friend — just the other side of a course of brick
a child could scratch through. I did it: three days with a spoon and I was through, and now look at
me. Out. On the bright side.// A pause, too eager by half. //You could be here by Sunday. I'd not lie
to you. Why would I lie to a man so nearly out?// It does not say what stands where it is standing. It
does not say what the men who set this brick were keeping on //its// side. And the warmth under your
palm, you understand of a sudden, is your own heat, thrown back at you off a wall that goes nowhere
but further down.
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The mortar gives back only the small cold fact of itself now, and a warmth you have decided is the
wall's and not your own. Whatever is on the far side has stopped trying. It has all the time there is
to start again.
<</if>>

<<link "Back to the weir">><<goto "TheBoneWeir">><</link>>


:: TheInscription [upper]
A chamber off the north mouth, and the walls of it are carved — not the city's
stencilled hazard-script but real carving, cut deep by patient hands into the old
stone, courses and courses of it from floor to vault. Up near the top, where your
lamp barely reaches, it is recognisable: drains, levels, the honest diagram-language
of people who built water-works and were proud of them. But the carving goes down
the wall as the wall goes down in age, and as it descends it *changes*.

<<if $corruption gte 35>>
You can read the lower courses now. That is the thing you notice, and the thing you
wish you hadn't: a month ago, a life ago, they'd have been only marks. Now they
arrange themselves into sense as easily as your own alphabet, and the sense they
make is an instruction, and some animal floor of you has already started, faintly,
to obey.
<<else>>
The lowest courses are not engineering. They are not any language you know, and yet
the longer your eye rests on them the less they look written and the more they look
*grown* — as if down here, past a certain depth, the difference between a thing
carved and a thing that crawled stopped mattering, and the wall simply records
whatever the dark was thinking at that level. This is the hinge of the whole place.
Above it: plumbing. Below it: intent.
<</if>>

<<if not $readerKnown>>
A man stands with his nose almost to the lower courses, reading — a works-surveyor by the rotted
look of him, come to catalogue the carving and stayed to read past the line where it stops being
carving. The filth has tinged him the colour of the stone. He does not turn, but he knows you are
there.
<<linkreplace "Ask him what the lower courses say">><<nobr>><<set $readerKnown to true>>
//It's a ledger,// he says, tracing a line you cannot read. //That's what they never grasp, up top:
they think the filth just rots. It doesn't rot. It //agrees.// Every harmless ruin that ever washed
down here puts its mark to the same page — a dead rat, a dropped coin, a man who sat down in the
dark — and the marks add up to a thing none of them was: a will, bigger and meaner than the sum of
its parts, pooled in the deep and reading back up the wall.// He glances at you at last, and there
is stone in the glance. //You've started reading it too. That's the first mark you put to the page.//
<</nobr>><<corrupt "head" "filth" 3>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
He has moved further down the wall, mouthing a lower line; he will not be drawn back up to where the
courses still make the kind of sense a person can walk away from.
<</if>>

<<link "Deeper, where the walls run wet">><<goto "TheDripstone">><</link>>
<<link "A root-broken cellar, off the side">><<goto "TheRootCellar">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the great sink">><<goto "TheGreatSink">><</link>>


:: TheCisternOverflow [upper]
An overflow cistern, vaulted like a sunken church, built to take the surge when the
old drains ran full and to give it back slow. It is half-full now of black still
water, and the vault throws your lamp back at you doubled off its surface, so you
stand at the rim between two identical dark rooms, the real one and the drowned one,
and cannot for a long uneasy moment say which way is the floor.

You edge along the rim and the second room edges with you, perfect, patient, its
vault hung below the water like the same vault dropped down a well. The pillars go
down into it and meet their own reflections at the waterline, so that each one is a
single great column pinched in the middle, neither end of it standing on anything
you can find. Somewhere out across the black the water is so still it holds the
ceiling's far corners better than your lamp can reach them, and you have to keep
reminding yourself that the drowned room has no floor, that it is only the dark
agreeing to look like depth.

A drip falls somewhere with a sound far too large for a drip, and the doubled room
shivers and stills, and you have the strong sense that the reflection took a moment
longer than you did to go quiet.

<<link "A dead siphon, beyond the overflow">><<goto "TheBrokenSiphon">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the dead-letter drain">><<goto "TheDeadLetterDrain">><</link>>


:: TheBrokenSiphon [upper]
The passage ends at a siphon that broke its back a long time ago — a great inverted
U of pipe meant to carry the flow under some old obstruction, cracked now along its
crown and silted solid, going nowhere, draining nothing. Someone sheltered here once
and didn't leave: there is a nest of rotted cloth in the dry curve of it, and a tin
cup, and the patient suggestion of a person who sat down in the one dry spot to wait
out a thing that does not end.

There is nothing here to take and nothing here to do but understand, a little
better than you'd like, how a person ends up sitting down in the dark for good.

<<if not $sitterTalked>>
The nest is not quite empty. What sat down here is still here: a man the drains have begun to cure
into something that keeps, hunched in the dry curve, the tin cup moving in a hand that remembers
being a hand. He is no threat and no bargain. He has one thing to tell you.
<<linkreplace "Crouch by the rag-nest; the cup-hand moves">><<nobr>><<set $sitterTalked to true>>
//You're still going down, then,// he says, not unkindly. //I went down once. Met a thing in the
wet — a polite sort of thing; it would have let me by — but my back wouldn't make the bow it wanted.
The drains had set it crooked already. I couldn't bend like a person any more, so I came back up to
the one dry spot and sat.// The cup turns and turns. //Mind that. Down in the deep the polite thing
beats the strong thing — but only while you can still //be// polite. Stay bendable, friend. It's the
last thing to go, and the one you'll wish you'd kept.//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
He has gone back to facing the dry pipe-wall, the cup still in his hand; he said his piece, and
there is nothing in the broken siphon now but the patience that kept him.
<</if>>

<<link "Back to the overflow cistern">><<goto "TheCisternOverflow">><</link>>


:: TheTanneryDrain [upper]
<<corrupt "torso" "filth" 2>>
A side-channel runs in from the west, and the stink of it stands in the air like a
wall you have to walk through: the specific gut-deep reek of an old tannery's
runoff, of hides and lime and the brown chemistry of turning skin into something
that lasts. The works that fed it are long dust, but the drain remembers; the silt
is the colour of old leather, and it has tanned the timbers and the rats and, you
realise touching the rail, the rail, so that everything the channel touches goes
slowly to hide.

Your own hand, where it rested on the rail, smells of it now, and won't stop, and
some cold forward part of you notes that this is how it works down here — not all
at once, but a channel at a time, a touch at a time, the place curing you into
something that will keep.

<<if not $tannerCured>>
A leathered remnant of the old tannery crew works the channel — skin gone to worked hide, hands like
tool-handles — and offers, in the brown reek, to cure a patch of you the way the works cured
everything it touched: toughened, lasting, rot-proof. It would keep, he says. It would last.
<<link "Let him cure a patch of you proper — it'll keep">>
<<set $tannerCured to true>><<corrupt "torso" "filth" 12>>
<<goto "TheSumpGalleries">>
<</link>>
<<link "Keep your skin your own, and walk on">>
<<soothe 4>>
<<goto "TheGreatSink">>
<</link>>
<<else>>
The cured patch of you creaks when you move, stiff and slick and not quite yours; the tanner has
nothing more to give a hide already turned, and works the channel without looking up.

<<link "On, into branching galleries">><<goto "TheSumpGalleries">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the great sink">><<goto "TheGreatSink">><</link>>
<</if>>


:: TheRootCellar [deep]
The brick gives way here to something the brick was only ever a lid on: a cellar of
older make, its ceiling broken open by roots. Great pale roots, thick as a thigh,
have come down through the stone from some tree that cannot possibly be growing
where they say it is — there is no soil up there, no light, nothing a tree could
want — and yet down they come, blind and patient and faintly *warm* to the touch,
pulsing very slowly, on a schedule that is not the schedule of water in pipes.

Moving in among them, you see they have not so much broken the masonry as *digested*
it — the brick courses where they pass through are bowed, drawn inward, the mortar
sucked thin, the stone fitted around the root the way a gum grows around a tooth.
The smaller hairs of them have found the cracks and gone in fine as thread, and at
their tips, deep in the seams, the stone is wet and dark and softened, going back
to the soil it was fired out of. Whatever is up there is not climbing down toward
you. It is feeding, and the cellar is the meal, and you are standing in the dish.

You do not want to know what the tree is, or where, or what it has put its roots
down through all this masonry to reach. You step around them carefully, the way you
would step around a sleeping animal, and they do not wake, and that is somehow the
worst of it: that they could.

<<link "Through, toward water that sings">><<goto "TheDripstone">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the chamber of carving">><<goto "TheInscription">><</link>>


:: TheDripstone [deep]
The walls run wet here, and the wet has built things — flowstone, the slow lime
architecture of dripping water, sheets and columns and frozen cascades of stone
poured one drop at a time across a span that makes the old masonry look new. Except
the city's filth has fed it too, so the formations are not the clean amber of a cave
but a marbled horror of grey and rust and a sick translucent yellow, and the water
that built them still falls, drop by drop, ringing in the dark with a clarity that
is almost, but not quite, a tune.

You stand and listen longer than you mean to. The drip is random — you know the
drip is random — but the part of you that has been down here too long keeps hearing
the next note coming, and keeps being right, and that is a thing you decide, firmly,
to stop noticing before you start humming along.

<<if not $dripstoneHeard>>
You have decided to stop noticing the not-quite-tune when you notice, under it, that the drip is
keeping time with something — a voice, almost, part of the falling water, unhurried, and almost glad
of you.
<<linkreplace "The drip has a rhythm. Under the rhythm, almost, a voice — listen">><<nobr>><<set $dripstoneHeard to true>>
//You hear it too. The next one, before it falls. Don't be ashamed — everyone hears it; that's not
the trick.// The voice is the dripping, patient as stone. //The trick is the //relief.// The drip
was a stranger, and now it isn't, and being right about a stranger feels like company down here —
and company is the one thing the deep can't sell you, so it gives it away. Stay. Match it. It's so
much easier than keeping your own count.// A drop falls, exactly when you knew it would. //And
keeping your own count, friend, was the last thing you had that was yours.//
<</nobr>><<corrupt "head" "filth" 3>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The drip is only water again, or you have decided to hear it that way; the voice in it has nothing
left to add, having told you already the one thing it knew.
<</if>>

<<link "Down, toward the sump galleries">><<goto "TheSumpGalleries">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the chamber of carving">><<goto "TheInscription">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the root-broken cellar">><<goto "TheRootCellar">><</link>>


:: TheSumpGalleries [deep]
The lowest reach of the old drains is a maze of sump-galleries, low brick tunnels
that branch and rejoin and branch again with the logic of a thing that grew toward
water rather than a thing that was drawn on paper. They all run very slightly
downhill. Whichever way you turn, you can feel the floor tilting you, gently,
patiently, toward the lower dark — toward the old stair and the flooded places
under it — the way the whole of this layer tilts, the way everything down here
tilts, toward the bottom of the world.

<<if $sumpRatCleared>>
In a blind side-bay the rat-men's den lies broken open, the shredded-rag bedding
gone still and cooling, the drowned that lodged with them properly still at last
in its seep. They denned at the bottom because the galleries all run down to them;
the down-running dark is yours to walk alone now.
<<else>>
One blind side-bay is a den, and the things in it are the upright kind — rat-men,
the mutagen finished in them, standing on their hind legs with their too-many-
knuckled hands full of what they've dragged down here to gnaw. The biggest holds
the mouth of the bay and does not rush you; the galleries all run downhill to this
spot, and it knows it. Behind it, in the bedding, the dull gleam of things worth
having. And behind //that//, where the bay dips to standing water, something else
has come up the running dark to lodge among them — a drowned, sat quiet in the seep,
tolerated the way the deep tolerates everything that washes in: it drained here, so
it belongs here. It stands when the big one does.
<</if>>

<<if $deepTurns gte setup.SEAL_WARN and not $sealed>>
You have been a long time in the branching now, and the galleries have begun to
repeat — or you have — and the small downhill pull of the floor is starting to feel
less like gravity and more like a hand at the small of your back.
<</if>>

<<if not $sumpRatCleared>>
<<scentof `["ratman","drowned"]`>>
<<link "Take the big rat-man, clear the den">>
  <<run setup.startFight(["ratman", "drowned"], "TheSumpGalleriesCleared")>>
  <<goto "Combat">>
<</link>>
<</if>>
<<link "A bed of warm silt, in a side-bay">><<goto "TheWarmSilt">><</link>>
<<link "The old stair, going down">><<goto "TheOlderStair">><</link>>
<<link "Toward the dripstone chamber">><<goto "TheDripstone">><</link>>
<<link "Back the way you came, west to the tannery drain">><<goto "TheTanneryDrain">><</link>>


:: TheSumpGalleriesCleared [deep]
/* lands here after the rat-man fight; sets the flag, then folds back */
<<set $sumpRatCleared to true>>
The big rat-man goes over backward with its hands still full, and the rest of the
den scatters chittering into cracks too small to follow — they are brave only in a
pack, and the pack is only as brave as its biggest. You strip the bedding of the
coin and oddments they'd hoarded out of the down-running silt, and try not to think
about which of the gnawed things were once shaped like a hand.

<<link "Back to the branching galleries">><<goto "TheSumpGalleries">><</link>>


/* ---------- LAYER-3 BAD END ---------- */

:: EndingGrate [ending]
<<run setup.recordRun("taken")>>
You answer. Of course you answer — some part of you always answers, when the dark
takes the trouble to learn your private name and say it gently. You open your mouth
to tell it which side of the grate you're on, and the telling is the trick, because
the moment you reach for the answer you find you are no longer certain, and the
uncertainty is a door, and the door was the only thing the grate ever was.

The bars are behind you now. You don't remember passing through them; there was
nothing to pass through; there was only the slow understanding that the easier part
it promised is real, and you have earned it, and it is this: the not-deciding, the
not-being-afraid, the standing in the patient dark on whichever side of the grate
the voice is on, saying a tired and solitary stranger's name through the bars,
kindly, keeping their place.

//You answered the door. It only ever needed you to be unsure which side you were
on.//

<<link "Again">><<run setup.restartCarry()>><</link>>
